      "name": "_strcmp",
      "signature": "int _strcmp(char * lpszString1, char * lpszString2)",
      "calling_convention": "__stdcall",
      "comment": "CRT strcmp - Optimized string comparison function\n\nIMPORTANT: Function body requires manual fix in Ghidra.\nCurrent body: 0x6fa15d90 - 0x6fa15d90 (incorrect, should extend to ~0x6fa15e1f)\nInternal jumps exist at 0x6fa15dcc, 0x6fa15df6, 0x6fa15e12 proving code extends further.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Load lpszString1 into EDX from [ESP+4]\n2. Load lpszString2 into ECX from [ESP+8]\n3. Test EDX for 4-byte alignment (TEST EDX, 3)\n4. If unaligned, handle byte-by-byte comparison at step 8\n5. If aligned, compare 4 bytes at a time (DWORD comparison)\n6. For each DWORD: compare bytes 0-3 with OR checks for null terminator\n7. If all 4 bytes match and none null, advance pointers by 4, loop to step 5\n8. Byte-by-byte path: compare single characters until mismatch or null\n9. Return difference between mismatched characters or 0 if equal\n\nParameters:\n  lpszString1 (char *) - First null-terminated string to compare [ESP+4]\n  lpszString2 (char *) - Second null-terminated string to compare [ESP+8]\n\nReturns:\n  int - 0 if strings are equal\n      - Negative if lpszString1 < lpszString2 lexicographically\n      - Positive if lpszString1 > lpszString2 lexicographically\n\nSpecial Cases:\n  - Uses DWORD-aligned fast path when first pointer is 4-byte aligned\n  - Falls back to byte-by-byte for unaligned strings or near null terminator\n  - Standard C library strcmp implementation with MSVC optimization\n  - Empty strings (both null at position 0) return 0",
      "name_source": "LoD/1.07",
